1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,553
High in the Andes of Peru, at Quehue, a bridge of grass rope crosses a gorge, about fifteen metres above the Apurímac river.

2
00:00:13,653 --> 00:00:17,779
Four communities look after it, and hold that its life depends on their care.

3
00:00:19,479 --> 00:00:23,870
By their own account, people have crossed here on rope since the time of the Incas.

4
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:30,541
But grass rope is out in the sun, the rain and the wind all year, and it weakens.

5
00:00:32,441 --> 00:00:39,025
Five months after one renewal, a visitor found the bridge sagging in the middle, one handrail lower than the other.

6
00:00:41,125 --> 00:00:46,064
A sagging bridge still carries people. Wait until it can't, and the sign is a fall.

7
00:00:48,164 --> 00:00:51,934
In 2020, in the pandemic, the bridge was not renewed.

8
00:00:53,634 --> 00:00:57,792
In March 2021, at twenty-one months old, it fell into the river.

9
00:01:00,092 --> 00:01:05,178
People who cross a gorge on rope need it renewed before it fails, not repaired after.

10
00:01:07,278 --> 00:01:12,416
So the communities don't wait for that. Each year, on a date, they make the whole bridge new.

11
00:01:14,316 --> 00:01:16,826
About a thousand people weave it in three days.

12
00:01:18,326 --> 00:01:24,433
In June 2021, they wove it again, with their own means, and people cross it on foot today.

13
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:32,345
Its rope lasts a year or two. By its people's account, the bridge has lasted more than five hundred years.

14
00:01:34,445 --> 00:01:40,516
The way a team's work is arranged is a bridge like that. People stand on it every day, and it wears.

15
00:01:42,416 --> 00:01:45,348
Wait for that wear to show, and it shows in a person.

16
00:01:47,248 --> 00:01:51,183
Renew it on a date instead, and people stay well while the work gets done.

17
00:01:59,883 --> 00:02:04,787
At the university, it is February, and Helena Novak is the new data manager.

18
00:02:06,287 --> 00:02:13,115
Fourteen of the team's forty-six open requests wait on one person: Marta Silva, its only analyst.

19
00:02:15,015 --> 00:02:17,770
Each was called urgent by the person who asked.

20
00:02:19,070 --> 00:02:22,938
Nobody decides their order, so whoever asked last comes first.

21
00:02:24,638 --> 00:02:30,700
Nobody is to blame. The requests came in eleven ways, so the people asking couldn't see each other's.

22
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:37,376
Helena could ask Marta to cope: to plan better, to push back, to take a day off.

23
00:02:39,076 --> 00:02:41,624
The fourteen would still be there when she came back.

24
00:02:43,524 --> 00:02:46,433
So Helena looks at the work before she looks at the person.

25
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:55,760
On Monday the eighth, in their weekly half-hour, Marta and Helena write Marta's last week on one page.

26
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:01,005
They take it from her inbox and her calendar, not from memory.

27
00:03:02,505 --> 00:03:08,142
One side says what the work asks: the fourteen, all urgent, in an order she has no say in.

28
00:03:10,042 --> 00:03:16,201
She picked up nine of them that week and finished none. Each time she turned to one, someone asked about another.

29
00:03:18,301 --> 00:03:20,724
And in January, she worked two Sundays.

30
00:03:22,624 --> 00:03:29,923
The other side says what helps. She knows the university's numbers better than anyone, and Jun checks a query when she asks.

31
00:03:31,823 --> 00:03:35,847
Researchers call the two sides job demands and job resources.

32
00:03:37,747 --> 00:03:42,738
What wears people down is mostly in how the work is arranged. So is what protects them.

33
00:03:47,638 --> 00:03:54,217
Now read the page against three things everyone needs from work: a say in it, to be good at it, and to belong.

34
00:03:56,517 --> 00:04:03,172
Marta has no say in her order. She is good at the work, and finishes little of it. And she is alone with the fourteen.

35
00:04:05,472 --> 00:04:09,911
Two psychologists, Richard Ryan and Edward Deci, named these needs.

36
00:04:11,611 --> 00:04:18,912
In hundreds of studies since, at work, in school and in sport, people supported in them are better off, and do somewhat better.

37
00:04:21,012 --> 00:04:23,835
Meet those needs, and people care about the work.

38
00:04:25,735 --> 00:04:32,301
Taking away what annoys them is not the same thing. A faster laptop gives Marta no say in her order.

39
00:04:34,401 --> 00:04:38,158
And nothing on the page is about Marta. All of it is about the work.

40
00:04:43,258 --> 00:04:48,518
On Wednesday the tenth, Helena takes the page to the nine people who asked, in one room.

41
00:04:50,418 --> 00:04:55,412
She brings it as evidence, with no blame. None of them had seen the others' requests.

42
00:04:57,312 --> 00:05:03,340
She comes prepared. The team wants one order, three requests at a time, and no weekends in the plan.

43
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:11,605
They want their number, by their date. Both want numbers to rely on, on a date they can plan around.

44
00:05:13,905 --> 00:05:19,069
The least she can accept is an agreed order, whatever it is, with a date for each request.

45
00:05:21,169 --> 00:05:27,699
In half an hour, they agree. Two requests are no longer needed. The other twelve get an order and a date each.

46
00:05:29,999 --> 00:05:34,332
From Thursday, Marta works on three at a time. Three is her own number.

47
00:05:36,232 --> 00:05:41,326
For now, a new request goes to Helena, who places it in the order with whoever asked.

48
00:05:46,626 --> 00:05:53,877
Back at the gorge, in 1972. Left for a time, the bridge still hung, whole to look at, gleaming like gold.

49
00:05:55,777 --> 00:06:00,689
Its keeper stopped two visitors at the edge. Don't cross, he said. The bridge is dying.

50
00:06:02,989 --> 00:06:05,170
It looked sound to them. He knew its rope.

51
00:06:07,070 --> 00:06:12,996
A manager can learn where to look in the work: how much is in hand at once, and who decides its order.

52
00:06:14,696 --> 00:06:21,806
How often people are interrupted. Who is alone on something. Nights and weekends. What is unclear. Who thanks whom.

53
00:06:23,906 --> 00:06:30,790
Safe Work Australia lists such things as hazards at work: job demands, low job control, poor support.

54
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:41,173
In people, there are three signs to notice early: worn out, distant from the job, and feeling no longer good at it.

55
00:06:43,273 --> 00:06:49,866
They are the World Health Organization's signs of burn-out, which comes from stress at work that has not been managed.

56
00:06:51,566 --> 00:06:55,678
It classes burn-out as a matter of work, not as a medical condition.

57
00:06:57,378 --> 00:07:02,272
So a manager who notices a sign asks, listens, and changes the work first.

58
00:07:03,972 --> 00:07:09,359
The same organization recommends that employers act directly on working conditions.

59
00:07:10,859 --> 00:07:14,518
And a manager's part has an end. Helena is not a clinician.

60
00:07:16,218 --> 00:07:20,297
When it is more than work, she points to professional help, and keeps it private.

61
00:07:23,597 --> 00:07:27,356
Helena doesn't wait for signs. She asks all seven about the work.

62
00:07:29,056 --> 00:07:34,085
On Friday the twelfth, she sends them the same five questions, to answer in writing.

63
00:07:36,185 --> 00:07:40,228
In writing, so that the quiet answer counts as much as the loud one.

64
00:07:42,128 --> 00:07:47,238
Three are about the needs. Two ask what wears you out, and what one change would help most.

65
00:07:49,338 --> 00:07:54,800
A week later, she shows all seven the answers, as counts and in the words each agreed to share.

66
00:07:56,700 --> 00:08:02,564
Two of seven have a say in their order. Three get to finish things. All seven have someone to turn to.

67
00:08:04,864 --> 00:08:10,971
What wears them out: the standing meetings, for five; requests with no order, for four; the nights, for one.

68
00:08:13,271 --> 00:08:19,673
She says what has changed already, Marta's order, and gives the rest a date: Monday the fifteenth of March.

69
00:08:24,973 --> 00:08:31,154
At Quehue, the old bridge is cut loose while it still holds. Nobody waits to see how long it would have lasted.

70
00:08:33,254 --> 00:08:39,293
What is made new there is the rope. In a team, it is the way the work is arranged, never the people.

71
00:08:42,193 --> 00:08:47,718
Helena's team has its date too. In June, she will ask the same five questions again.

72
00:08:49,618 --> 00:08:55,897
By the end of February, six of Marta's twelve are delivered, each on its date, and she has worked no Sunday.

73
00:08:58,197 --> 00:09:01,689
So this week, write one person's week on one page, with them.

74
00:09:03,589 --> 00:09:07,264
Take it to the people who ask, and agree a pace the team can keep.

75
00:09:09,164 --> 00:09:14,714
Ask everyone the same questions in writing, show the answers, and say when you will ask again.

76
00:09:16,614 --> 00:09:19,889
Change one thing in the work now, and put a date on the rest.

77
00:09:21,989 --> 00:09:25,379
The next film, No two alike, turns to how people differ.

78
00:09:27,079 --> 00:09:29,412
Fix the work before you ask people to cope.
